    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bedford Park was planned with 40 suburban homes on 23 acres, called Villa Avenue.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bedford_Park%2C_Bronx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The neighborhood was named Bedford Park after a London suburb, and developers built a suburban retreat 20 years after a sale.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nyc.gov/site/bronxcb7/about/bedford-park.page",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Bedford Park’s early streets were curved and marketed for healthful air near the then‑planning Bronx Park, home to the future zoo and botanical garden.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityneighborhoods.nyc/bedford-park",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1890s extension of the Third Avenue Elevated Line and the opening of Bedford Park Station on the Harlem Line anchored the community’s early accessibility.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityneighborhoods.nyc/bedford-park",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Lehman College, originally Hunter College’s uptown campus, sits on the Grand Concourse and is a notable landmark in Bedford Park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bedford_Park%2C_Bronx",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
